# GRUB boot-menu password for the kiosk image.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS
# Everything else in this image -- masked TTYs, disabled VT switching, locked
# accounts, Chromium policy -- is enforced by software that GRUB starts. A
# student who can edit the boot entry can append `init=/bin/sh` and get a root
# shell before any of it runs. A GRUB password is what closes that door.
#
# HOW TO SET ONE
#   grub-mkpasswd-pbkdf2
# Enter a password twice; it prints a line like:
#   PBKDF2 hash of your password is grub.pbkdf2.sha512.10000.ABC...123
# Copy the value starting at `grub.pbkdf2.sha512.` into PASSWORD_PBKDF2 below.
#
# The hash is safe to commit: it is a salted PBKDF2 digest, not the password.
# Keep the password itself with your other lab administration credentials.
#
# When this is set, the kiosk still boots its default entry with no prompt --
# only editing an entry or opening the GRUB command line asks for the password.
#
# Leaving it empty builds an image with an unprotected boot menu. That is fine
# for a VM or a bench test, but do not deploy it to a classroom that way.

PASSWORD_PBKDF2=
